{"type":"rewrite","text":"Libraries matter because they give students a quiet and supportive place to study. In a busy school or home, it is often difficult to find silence, but a library offers clean desks, comfortable chairs and an atmosphere of concentration. This environment helps students read more carefully, finish assignments on time and prepare for exams. Libraries also provide useful resources that students may not be able to afford, such as books, dictionaries, computers and reliable internet access. When students meet a difficult question, they can look for information in books or ask a librarian for help. In addition, libraries are fair places: rich and poor students can use the same facilities and enjoy the same chances to learn. They also teach students how to behave politely, share space and respect others who are studying. Therefore, libraries are not simply rooms full of books. They are important learning centres that help students gain knowledge, build good habits and become more independent. For these reasons, libraries will continue to matter to every student."}

