{"type":"rewrite","text":"Libraries widen access to knowledge while providing a quiet place to study. This dual role is important because knowledge should not be limited by income, age or background. A public library offers free books, newspapers, computers and internet access, so students from poor families and elderly people can learn at little cost. It also gives people a quiet environment in which to read, prepare for exams or finish work that cannot be done at home.\n\nHowever, access to libraries is not equal everywhere. Some communities have few libraries, and some people cannot reach them because of distance, disability or limited opening hours. To improve access, governments should fund local branches, extend opening hours and provide digital services such as online catalogues and e-books. Libraries should also offer help for people who are not confident with technology.\n\nIn short, libraries are not only storehouses of books but also bridges to knowledge and opportunity. If they are well funded and easy to reach, they can help more people learn, improve their skills and take part in society. Therefore, making libraries accessible is a wise investment in education and equality."}

