{"type":"rewrite","text":"Online feedback has become a common part of university study. Students can receive comments on a draft before the next class, and teachers can respond to questions that would otherwise remain unanswered. These opportunities are valuable, but the usefulness of feedback depends on how it is interpreted.\n\nA short response can provide useful information about a specific sentence or calculation. It cannot always explain why the student made the mistake. If learners copy a suggested answer without considering the reason behind it, they may make the same mistake in another task.\n\nStudents should therefore compare a comment with the original question, identify the part that needs attention and try to produce a new answer in their own words. When a suggestion seems unclear or conflicts with the course materials, asking the teacher is more useful than guessing what was intended.\n\nTeachers also have a role to play. Clear comments and a few clear priorities can help students focus their effort. In my view, online feedback works best as the beginning of a conversation, followed by revision and independent practice, rather than as a final judgement of their ability."}

