{"type":"rewrite","text":"A university needs spaces for both quiet concentration and informal conversation. Students do not all work in the same way, and their needs can change during the day. A successful campus should recognise these differences instead of expecting one room to serve every purpose.\n\nQuiet study areas allow students to read demanding material, prepare for examinations and work without interruption. Social spaces support discussion and help new students form friendships. If these activities are placed in a poorly planned shared area, ordinary conversation may disturb readers, while requests for silence may discourage useful exchange.\n\nOne practical solution is to separate spaces by purpose and make the rules clear and visible. A library could reserve some floors for silent work while offering rooms where small groups can discuss a project. Students should also have a way to report problems and suggest improvements, since a rule that looks sensible on paper may prove inconvenient in practice.\n\nThe aim is to provide consistent choices. By reviewing how rooms are used and listening to different groups, a university can protect concentration without treating social activity as a nuisance. Both forms of campus life deserve thoughtful support."}

